  message: "PR-2 of 3 LANDED (PR #409, dev 7afcf2f2d9): Site-1 self.method() inherited resolution. py.py's terminal unresolved-emit else stamps meta['enclosing_class'] (caller method's direct class, qualified_name.split('.')[-2]) as a leading mutually-exclusive elif; the inherited_calls _resolve_site1 (Python C3 walker from PR-1) mints the resolved edge. Design + adversarial-review workflows (wf_6991b0ea-f64 design, wf_e6f81f04-d63 review). The review (NO_GO -> fixed) caught TWO real defects I introduced: (1) MAJOR Ruby regression — reusing _LEGACY_SITE2_LANGS={java} for the Site-1 guard swept in Ruby, whose reopened classes (one logical class, many symbols) tripped len>1 and suppressed the INV-nilud-validated X.new->inherited #initialize resolution; fixed by scoping the guard to a dedicated _SITE1_STRICT_LANGS={python} (module-namespace langs only). (2) annotated self:T confidently-wrong 0.90 edge — the leading self-branch preempted an explicit self-annotation (mixin/host idiom); fixed by adding 'receiver_name not in var_types' so annotated self routes to Site-2 on T. Also added a staticmethod-with-self-param gate ('staticmethod' not in decorators). Behavioral evidence: +165 calls edges on self-analysis (62839->63004); cross-file Sub.run->Base.helper resolves; reopened Ruby still resolves; annotated self routes to Site-2. Both packages 100% cov; taint+ratchet green. STAYS OPEN — remaining: PR-3 = Site-3 self.field.method() (inherited_field_receiver + meta['fields'] population, mirror java.py:2382-2388). Filed follow-ups: cls.method() classmethod-inheritance gap; py.py .name.split('.')[0] harmonization."  # e8fd
